      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Rupture of an unscarred uterus is a rare and potentially catastrophic event. Common presenting signs and symptoms include abdominal pain, fetal heart rate abnormalities, and evidence of hypovolemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A woman with a history of 2 prior uncomplicated first-trimester pregnancy terminations presented several years later at 32 weeks' gestation with abdominal pain and no other evidence of uterine rupture. Her clinicians suspected appendicitis, and computed tomography revealed a circular fundal uterine rupture occluded by extrusion of the fetal legs through the defect. Emergent cesarean delivery resulted in a good outcome for mother and baby."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Clinicians should consider uterine rupture as a possible diagnosis when patients present with abdominal pain, even without common risk factors linical evidence of a ruptured uterus."
        }
      ]},
